page('guide.html','How to test stick drift and verify a repair','Learn how StickCheck measures resting stick movement, what the numbers mean, and how to make before-and-after repair tests comparable.', '''
<p class="eyebrow">THE STICKCHECK FIELD GUIDE</p><h1>A useful test starts<br>with a fair comparison.</h1><p class="lead">Measure what your controller reports. Understand the limits. Save evidence you can actually use.</p><section id="connection"><h2>01. Connect and check the setup</h2><ol><li>Open StickCheck directly in a current desktop browser over HTTPS. USB is a useful starting point; Bluetooth support depends on your device, operating system, and browser.</li><li>Connect the controller, click the page, and press a controller button. Browsers may wait for interaction before exposing a connected gamepad.</li><li>Choose the correct controller if more than one is connected. Set the connection type yourself; the browser does not reliably report it.</li><li>Move each stick separately. Verify the correct plot responds. For a non-standard mapping, set the X/Y axis numbers in “Axis mapping & compatibility.”</li><li>If it does not appear, try a data-capable USB cable, another port, and another supported browser. Close controller remapping software if it interferes, noting any setting changes before comparing tests.</li></ol><a class="button primary" href="/">Open diagnostics →</a></section>
<section id="repeat"><h2>02. Run a repeatable resting test</h2><p>Set the controller on a stable surface. Start the test and release both sticks during the three-second countdown. Do not touch or bump the controller during the following ten-second sample window. Keep the tab and window active; the test cancels when they lose focus.</p><p>Run the test two or three times. A consistent result is more informative than an isolated reading. Record the connection, browser, firmware, and any remapping or deadzone settings. Keep them the same for the after-repair test. Sampling follows browser frames; this is not a measurement of USB polling rate or end-to-end input latency.</p></section>
<section id="results"><h2>03. Read the numbers</h2><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Measurement</th><th>What it tells you</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Center offset</td><td>Distance of the average X/Y position from the mathematical center. Opposite-direction movement can cancel in this average.</td></tr><tr><td>95th percentile</td><td>95% of sampled radial offsets are at or below this value. Used for the reference check.</td></tr><tr><td>Peak offset</td><td>Largest sampled radial distance. Useful for spotting spikes, including accidental touches.</td></tr><tr><td>Jitter (RMS)</td><td>Root-mean-square distance from the average position. A measure of movement around the reported center.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>For each sample, radial offset = √(X² + Y²). The browser provides axis values between −1 and +1. We multiply distances by 100 to express a percentage of one-axis full scale. A diagonal position can therefore exceed 100%.</p><p>The reference deadzone defaults to 10% as an adjustable comparison aid. It is <strong>not a universal acceptable-drift threshold</strong>, does not change your controller settings, and is not necessarily equivalent to a game's deadzone shape or scale. “Above reference” means this measurement exceeded your selected reference—not that a repair is automatically needed.</p><p>Firmware, drivers, remappers, and operating-system processing can filter the signal before the browser receives it. A zero reading cannot establish that the physical sensor has zero drift. This tool cannot inspect internal hardware, calibrate controller firmware, or certify a repair.</p></section>
<section><h2>04. Save before. Compare after.</h2><p>Save a real resting test as your baseline and download its JSON report. The browser keeps one baseline locally, but clearing site data or using another device can remove access to it. Import your downloaded report to restore it.</p><p>After repair, repeat the test using the same physical controller and setup. StickCheck compares the device label, browser, connection type, axes, duration, and reference value. Browser device labels are not unique serial numbers, so you must confirm it is the same controller. A different firmware or deadzone can change the result even if the setup check matches.</p><p>The comparison reports the change in 95th-percentile resting offset in percentage points. Lower values indicate less resting movement under the measured conditions. Also move both sticks through their travel, release them from several directions, test every button and trigger, and try your usual games. A resting test alone cannot verify a complete repair.</p></section>
<section><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><details><summary>Does this work with PS5 and Xbox controllers?</summary><p>It works with controllers exposed by your browser's Gamepad API. Standard-mapped DualSense and Xbox controllers commonly use axes 0/1 and 2/3. Compatibility varies by operating system, connection method, and browser. Verify movement before interpreting a result.</p></details><details><summary>Can this fix my stick drift?</summary><p>No. StickCheck measures reported inputs. It does not write firmware, perform hardware calibration, or change a game’s settings. Use the controller manufacturer's guidance for those steps.</p></details><details><summary>What if my controller only drifts in one game?</summary><p>Compare that game's controller settings and deadzone with another game. A browser test sees a different input path and may not reproduce the issue. Describe the exact conditions to a repair provider.</p></details><details><summary>Why did the test cancel when I switched tabs?</summary><p>Browsers can reduce or pause sampling in inactive tabs. Cancelling avoids presenting a partial ten-second test as a complete measurement.</p></details><details><summary>Do you measure latency or actual polling rate?</summary><p>No. Display refresh and browser scheduling are different from the controller's hardware report rate and input-to-display latency. StickCheck does not make those measurements.</p></details></section>
<section><h2>Methodology sources</h2><p>Implementation references: <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Gamepad_API/Using_the_Gamepad_API" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MDN: Using the Gamepad API</a>, <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Gamepad/axes" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">axis values</a>, and <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Gamepad/mapping" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">controller mapping</a>. Methodology reviewed October 11, 2026.</p></section>''')
page('repair.html','Controller repair options','Compare independent controller repair services, check warranty coverage, and know what to ask before a stick drift repair.', '''
<p class="eyebrow">REPAIR, WITH A LITTLE MORE CONFIDENCE</p><h1>Keep your controller<br>in the game.</h1><p class="lead">Start with your warranty. Then compare repair options with your test results in hand.</p><div class="notice"><strong>Independent directory, not a partner network.</strong><br>These are ordinary links. StickCheck currently earns no commission from them. We have not independently tested these providers' workmanship. Check eligibility, total costs, shipping coverage, and current terms directly.</div>
<section><h2>Check warranty support first</h2><p>A paid third-party repair may not be your best first step. Check the manufacturer's support options and your purchase warranty before opening or shipping the controller.</p><p><a href="https://www.playstation.com/support/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PlayStation support ↗</a> &nbsp; · &nbsp; <a href="https://support.xbox.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Xbox support ↗</a> &nbsp; · &nbsp; <a href="https://support.nintendo.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Nintendo support ↗</a></p></section>
<section><h2>Independent mail-in repair options</h2><p>Providers below are US-oriented options. Confirm service availability and shipping from your location. Listings are alphabetical, not a quality ranking. Information checked October 11, 2026.</p><div class="card-grid">
<article class="resource-card"><p class="micro">CONTROLLER REPAIR & UPGRADES</p><h3>Battle Beaver</h3><p>Offers send-in services for eligible stock first-party controllers and its own controllers. Its service page lists restrictions on third-party modifications; check eligibility before sending anything.</p><a href="https://support.battlebeavercustoms.com/hc/en-us/articles/5346404223636-Send-In-Services-Controller-In-Need-of-Repairs-or-Upgrades-We-Can-Help" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-provider="battle-beaver">Check eligibility & services ↗</a></article>
<article class="resource-card"><p class="micro">STICK DRIFT & CONTROLLER REPAIR</p><h3>StickFix Repair</h3><p>Lists stick-drift repairs and controller upgrades, including services for modified controllers. Select the specific model and ask which repair or replacement module suits your issue.</p><a href="https://stickfixrepair.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-provider="stickfix">See repair services ↗</a></article>
<article class="resource-card"><p class="micro">MAIL-IN CONTROLLER REPAIR</p><h3>TechRevive</h3><p>Lists mail-in repairs for stick drift, buttons, charging, and triggers. Confirm your exact controller model, diagnosis process, and quote before placing an order.</p><a href="https://techrevivestore.com/collections/repair-service" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-provider="techrevive">See mail-in options ↗</a></article>
<article class="resource-card"><p class="micro">DO-IT-YOURSELF RESOURCES</p><h3>iFixit</h3><p>Repair guides, tools, and replacement parts for DIY repair. This is not a mail-in service. Check model compatibility and the tools and soldering skills required before choosing a guide.</p><a href="https://www.ifixit.com/Device/Computer_Game_Controller" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-provider="ifixit">Explore controller guides ↗</a></article></div></section>
<section><h2>Five questions to ask before paying</h2><ol><li><strong>Is my exact model eligible?</strong> Include the model number and any previous modifications.</li><li><strong>What is the complete cost?</strong> Ask about diagnosis, parts, calibration, tax, and shipping both ways.</li><li><strong>What work will you perform?</strong> Ask whether the quote covers cleaning, sensor replacement, full module replacement, or another repair.</li><li><strong>How do you verify the repair?</strong> Ask for resting position, return-to-center and range checks, and button/trigger testing.</li><li><strong>What happens if the problem returns?</strong> Confirm turnaround time, repair warranty, exclusions, and return-shipping responsibility.</li></ol></section>
<section><h2>Take your baseline with you.</h2><p>A downloaded test report gives you a record of the behavior before repair. Describe when you notice the problem in actual games too; a browser measurement is only one part of the story.</p><a class="button primary" href="/">Test and save a baseline →</a></section>
<section><h2>How links are selected</h2><p>We include providers with publicly described controller repair services relevant to this diagnostic tool. We do not claim affiliation, certification, or a guarantee of results. If we introduce paid referral links later, we will label them near the link and update our disclosure. Payment will not change your test results.</p><a href="/privacy.html">Read the privacy and monetization disclosure ↗</a></section>''')
page('privacy.html','Privacy and monetization disclosure','How StickCheck handles diagnostic inputs, saved baselines, reports, external links, and future monetization.', '''
<p class="eyebrow">CLEAR BY DESIGN</p><h1>Your data.<br>Your controller.</h1><p class="lead">Privacy and monetization disclosure · Updated October 11, 2026.</p><section><h2>Controller data stays in your browser</h2><p>StickCheck reads gamepad axes, button values, and a browser-provided controller label to run diagnostics. The diagnostic code does not upload these readings. Live movement trails are kept in memory and discarded when you leave.</p></section><section><h2>Saved baselines and downloaded reports</h2><p>If you choose “Save as before-repair baseline,” we store one report in localStorage on this browser. It includes the reported device label, browser user-agent, selected connection type and axes, test time, reference setting, and summary measurements. Delete it with the baseline delete button or your browser's site-data controls.</p><p>Downloaded JSON reports contain the same information and may include your saved baseline. Review the file before sharing it with anyone. Imports are processed locally. Demo results are explicitly marked and cannot be imported as a real baseline.</p></section><section><h2>Hosting and fonts</h2><p>The hosting service processes normal web requests and may maintain operational logs. Access to this private version is controlled by the hosting platform. The website uses locally bundled fonts; its diagnostic code includes no analytics or advertising trackers.</p></section><section><h2>External providers</h2><p>Repair-directory links open independent websites with their own privacy policies, service terms, and data handling. Your report is not automatically shared when you follow a link.</p></section><section><h2>Advertising and affiliate disclosure</h2><p>No AdSense ads or paid affiliate links are active in this version. The repair directory uses ordinary links and we currently earn no commission from them. We may apply to advertising and affiliate programs later. Any activated referral links will carry a clear nearby commission disclosure; advertising integrations and related privacy information will be updated before activation.</p></section><section><h2>About this tool</h2><p>StickCheck is an independent browser-based diagnostic tool, not a controller manufacturer or repair service. Results describe browser-reported input under the test conditions. They do not certify a controller as fault-free or guarantee that a repair is necessary or successful.</p></section><a class="button secondary" href="/">Back to diagnostics →</a>''')
p=root/'.openai/hosting.json';d=json.loads(p.read_text());d['static']={'directory':'dist'};p.write_text(json.dumps(d,indent=2)+'\n')
